"comparison": "Both Citi and Goldman Sachs disclose a hierarchy-based fair value measurement policy that prioritizes observable market inputs. Citi classifies items in the hierarchy based on the lowest significant input level, explicitly noting that an item may be Level 3 even with some observable inputs (c_2024_7_P669_S005). Similarly, Goldman Sachs states that assets and liabilities are classified in their entirety based on the lowest level of input significant to their fair value measurement (gs_2024_7_P016_S005) and defines the U.S. GAAP hierarchy priority, noting that substantially all financial assets and liabilities are classified in levels 1 and 2 based on observable prices (gs_2024_7_P017_S001).",
